### Coding Tips for AI engineers 

Covers everything from refactoring, readability, to testing, reproducibility, and collaboration (i.e. key elements to become production ready)
- Refactoring (i.e. changing structure and organisation of code for improved readability and scale but without changing substance or behaviour)
    - Keep functions short and focused (single responsibility)
    - Name things well (saves more time than comments)
    - Breakdown long scripts into modules (e.g. data_utils.py, modeling.py, viz.py)
    - Avoid code duplication: reuse functions 
    - Clean up old code after testing 
- Write Tests Early using frameworks such as unittest
    - Unit test your 
        - Feature engineering functions 
        - Model output shapes and ranges 
        - API endpoints (e.g. prediction services) 
- Organise project properly to help with reusability, teamwork and scaling by following standard structure such as 
my_project/ 
    - data/
    - notebooks/
    - src/ 
        - data.utils.py
        - modeling.py
        - inference.py
    - tests/
    - main.py
    - requirements.txt
    - README.md
    -.gitignore (this is a special file used in Git projects to tell Git which files or folders to ignore i.e. not track/include in version control. Usually includes config files, secret files, compiled code, virtual environments etc)
- Write Clean, Documented Code 
    - Use docstrings for all functions """ insert_doc_string_here """
    - Add inline comments when necessary, but don't overload 
    - Use type hints for better readability and editor support 
        - E.g. def predict(x: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray: 
- Track Experiments Like a Scientist to ensure results are reproducible and reviewable 
    - Use MLflow or Weights & Biases to log (i) Model params, (ii) Metrics, (iii) Dataset versions, (iv) Git commit hashes 
    - MLflow and Weights & Biases are both Python libraries that come with a web interface (UI) 
        - Can use them in Python scripts or notebooks, and they automatically log your experiments. 
        - Can then open a local or cloud dashboard to view everything 
    - Workflow (MLflow)
        - Install: pip install mlflow 
        - Add to training script: import mlflow, mlflow.sklearn 
        - Start autologging: mlflow.sklearn.autolog() #logs parameters, metrics, artifacts automatically 
        - Initialise: with mlflow.start_run(): 
                        model = SomeModel()
                        model.fit(X_train, y_train)
                        predictions = model.predict(X_test)
        - View in UI: mlflow ui 
            - Then go to local host (e.g. localhost5000 to view dashboard showing runs)
    - Workflow (W&B): 
        - pip install wandb
        - import wandb
        - wandb.init(project="my_model_project") #note if using Keras, PyTorch Lightning, HF etc it will hook into training loop automatically and log 
            - training and validation loss/accuracy, epochs/batch_size/LR, system metrics (GPU usage/RAM), model files/gradients etc
            - can also log Git commit hash + Python environment if enabled
        - Can still log things manually, e.g. 
            - wandb.config.learning_rate = 0.01
            - wandb.log({"accuracy": 0.92})
    - More information
        - MLflow is open source tool by Databricks
            - Logs parameters, metrics, artifacts 
            - Storage: local, S3 or hosted MLflow server 
            - UI: Web UI (basic)
            - Deployment tracking: Yes
            - Code integration: Python, R, Java 
            - Collaboration: Needs setup 
        - Weights & Biases: SaaS tool built by ex-Google Brain folks 
            - Logs parameters, metrics, artifacts and has nice built-in charts
            - Storage: W&B cloud or self-hosted
            - UI: sleek, interactive dashboards
            - Deployment tracking: Yes (plus model monitoring if pro)
            - Code integration: Python, Keras, PyTorch, XGBoost, more 
            - Collab: Ready out-of-box
- Use Git branches to Version Control everything 
    - Branches include 
        - main: production-ready
        - dev: in-progress
        - feature branches like feature/model-tuning 
    - Commit atomic, meaningful chunks of work with clear messages
    - Use .gitignore to avoid committing data, models, and logs 
- Think in terms of pipelines 
    - Break work into stages: ingest -> clean -> transform -> model -> serve 
    - Use DAG tools like Airflow or Prefect to schedule & track 
    - Aim for modular and composable code blocks 
- Automate boring stuff 
    - Use Makefile, nox, or tox for: 
        - Tests
        - Formatting (black, isort)
        - Linting (flake8, pylint)
    - Setup Github Actions to automate tests and checks on every push 
- Log, Monitor, and Visualize 
    - Use logging module - not just print()
    - Track input/output drift with tools like Evidently AI 
    - Built dashboards with Streamlist, Dash, or embed in notebooks 
- Respect Data Privacy & Ethics 
    - Never log or expose sensitive data
    - Build explainability into models (SHAP, LIME)
    - Regularly assess fairness, bias, and representativeness of data
